{
  "book": "MAT",
  "chapter": 23,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-213eb9a4ea3fe3a0": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-701",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This would be more correctly rendered by “strain out a gnat.” The at is supposed to have been originally a typographical error, which has since been universally copied. Alford, however, doubts this, and supposes that it “was a deliberate alteration, meaning, strain [out the wine]…"
      },
      "c-2f48fca5c31bcca6": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-702",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The tombs were whitened with lime, so that they could be easily distinguished, and thus prevent the Jews from being ceremonially defiled by approaching them. See Numbers 19:18. This whitewash became soiled and washed off by the rains, and it was therefore necessary to renew it at…"
      },
      "c-57ba8f35986f6998": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-703",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Jews not only whitewashed the tombs, but, in common with other peoples, they ornamented them in various ways. This is still customary in the East. The graves of the most eminent Mohammedan saints are each covered with a Wile or brick edifice called welee. It has a dome or cup…"
      },
      "c-7d5e8d7875b77827": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-697",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Whether the commands in Exodus 13:9,16; Deuteronomy 6:8; and 11:18, concerning the duty of binding the word upon the hand and head, were designed to be interpreted figuratively or literally, is a disputed point among commentators. The Jews have for ages attached to them a literal…"
      },
      "c-c6e8d19c1fd8a7f0": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-699",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This was a title of distinction given to teachers, and literally means Master, or Teacher. It is supposed to have been introduced during our Lord’s ministry. Lightfoot says: “We do not too nicely examine the precise time when this title began; be sure it did not commence before t…"
      },
      "c-c999af1695827eac": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-698",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 12:39; Luke 11:43; 20:43. These “chief seats” were seats of honor which were prepared for the elders of the synagogue and for the doctors of the law; and hence called, in the second verse of this chapter, “Moses’ seat.” They were placed in front of the ark, which co…"
      },
      "c-e0628876d391704e": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-700",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 12:40; Luke 20:47."
      }
    },
